# Byte-authoritative paths.
#
# The curriculum importer hashes the bytes it reads, and the published release
# manifest (docs/curriculum/a1-restructure/curriculum-release-v1.51-rc1.json)
# records SHA-256 over the *Git blob* bytes. With `core.autocrlf=true` a Windows
# checkout rewrites LF to CRLF, so the same commit hashed differently on Windows
# and Linux and `release.json` recorded a platform-dependent manifest.
#
# `-text` disables end-of-line conversion in both directions: the working tree
# gets the blob verbatim, and `git add` stores what is on disk unchanged. That
# makes the Git blob the single authority for these files on every platform.
# It deliberately does NOT normalise anything: the published v1.51 sequencing
# CSVs are stored with CRLF terminators and keep them, and the shared v1.37 and
# v1.40 inventory CSVs are stored with LF and keep that. No blob is rewritten,
# so no approved hash changes.
#
# `diff` is set alongside so these stay textually diffable rather than being
# reported as binary.
#
# Scope is deliberately narrow. There is no repo-wide `text=auto`: normalising
# every source file would churn the tree without making any hash reproducible.

content/a1/vocabulary/**/*.csv          -text diff
generated/curriculum/a1/*.json          -text diff
docs/curriculum/a1-restructure/*.csv    -text diff
docs/curriculum/a1-restructure/*.json   -text diff
